T*
(* Q1: Explain Python's memory management architecture, distinguishing between) Tj
T*
(reference counting and generational garbage collection.) Tj
T*
(Answer: CPython manages memory primarily through Reference Counting. Every Python object) Tj
T*
(header contains an ob_refcnt counter tracking how many references point to it. When an) Tj
T*
(assignment, function call, or list insertion binds to the object, ob_refcnt increments;) Tj
T*
(when a variable goes out of scope or is reassigned, ob_refcnt decrements. When ob_refcnt) Tj
T*
(drops to zero, memory is deallocated immediately. However, reference counting fails to) Tj
T*
(reclaim Circular References \(e.g. object A references B, and B references A\). To resolve) Tj
T*
(this, CPython incorporates a Generational Garbage Collector that segregates objects into) Tj
T*
(three generations \(Gen 0, 1, 2\) based on survival time and periodically sweeps through) Tj
T*
(pointers to break unreachable circular reference cycles.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Differentiate between the '==' operator and the 'is' operator in Python with) Tj
T*
(concrete illustrative examples.) Tj
T*
(Answer: The '==' operator evaluates Value Equality: it calls the __eq__\(\) dunder method) Tj
T*
(to check whether two distinct objects hold equivalent data content. The 'is' operator) Tj
T*
(evaluates Object Identity: it compares the raw memory addresses of two operands \(id\(a\)) Tj
T*
(== id\(b\)\) to check if both variable names point to the exact same physical heap object.) Tj
T*
(For example, if a = [1, 2] and b = [1, 2], a == b evaluates to True because their) Tj
T*
(element values match, but a is b evaluates to False because two independent list objects) Tj
T*
(were allocated at distinct memory locations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
